TI  - Dual-Layer Tenant Isolation in Shared-Schema SaaS: Combining ORM Filters with PostgreSQL Row-Level Security
T2  - IEEE Transactions on Cloud Computing
JO  - IEEE Transactions on Cloud Computing
PY  - 2026
VL  - XX
IS  - X
SP  - 1
EP  - 16
UR  - https://github.com/Varadha9/multi-tenant-saas-platform
AB  - Shared-schema multi-tenancy maximizes hardware consolidation and operational efficiency in SaaS platforms, but exposes systems to cross-tenant data leaks if application-level filtering fails. We present NexusOps, an enterprise SaaS platform implementing a dual-layer isolation architecture pairing Hibernate ORM filters with PostgreSQL forced Row-Level Security (RLS) under transaction-scoped session configuration (SET LOCAL). NexusOps enforces zero cross-tenant leakage across three automated guardrails: build-time schema AST inspection, eager database role validation ensuring the application role lacks BYPASSRLS, and transaction-lifecycle hooks preventing connection-pool session bleeding. We formalize tenant non-interference under recycled connection pools and evaluate the architecture across 1,000,000 records using a paired empirical benchmark harness (n = 30 runs per workload) with Two One-Sided Tests (TOST) for statistical equivalence. Forced RLS incurs bounded engine overhead between 2.3% and 8.8% over an application-filtered baseline across read, write, paginated, and join workloads, while preserving sub-millisecond median latencies (0.72-2.99 ms) and robust p99 tail bounds. Fault-injection tests against eight isolation mutants achieve a 100% detection rate.
KW  - Multi-Tenancy
KW  - Row-Level Security
KW  - Database Security
KW  - Software-as-a-Service
KW  - Hibernate ORM
KW  - PostgreSQL
KW  - Access Control
KW  - Defense-in-Depth
KW  - Empirical Benchmarking
KW  - TOST Equivalence
N1  - Key Highlights & NexusOps Synthesis: Core publication introducing the NexusOps dual-layer isolation architecture. Key highlights: (1) Theorem 1 formal proof of non-interference under recycled connection pools; (2) Properties 1-5 formal access guarantees; (3) Three automated CI/CD guardrails (AST inspector, eager role guard, ScopedValue/transaction hook); (4) 100% kill rate across 8 fault-injection isolation mutants; (5) Empirical evaluation over 1,000,000 records with n=30 paired runs proving RLS overhead is bounded between 2.3% and 8.8%; (6) TOST statistical equivalence within +/-10% corridor (p < 0.05); (7) Zero leaks across 10^6 requests (Hanley Rule-of-Three upper bound p_95% < 3x10^-6).
ID  - poonawala2026nexusops
L1  - main.pdf
